# Copy this file to `.env` and fill it in. Both `.env` and every `*.env`
# are gitignored, so nothing you put here can be committed by accident.
#
# Every field below names the SETUP.md section that tells you where its
# answer comes from. If a comment ever sends you to a section that does
# not exist, that is a bug worth an issue.
#
# The short path through all of this is QUICKSTART.md.

# --------------------------------------------------------------------
# ESPN -- which league to pull, and how to authenticate to it
# --------------------------------------------------------------------

# The number in your ESPN league URL. Required: the extract reads it at
# import time and cannot start without it.
#   SETUP.md section 3 -> "Find your league ID"
LEAGUE_ID=your_league_id_here

# Browser cookies, needed only for a PRIVATE league; a public one can
# leave both blank. They expire, so an extract that used to work and now
# returns nothing usually means these need refreshing.
#   SETUP.md section 3 -> "Find your cookies"
ESPN_S2=your_espn_s2_cookie_here
SWID=your_swid_cookie_here

# --------------------------------------------------------------------
# Snowflake -- where raw league data lands and where models are built
# --------------------------------------------------------------------

# If you have not created the database and warehouse yet, start there.
#   SETUP.md section 4 -> "Create the database and warehouse"
#
# These five come off the account you just created.
#   SETUP.md section 4 -> "Capture your connection details"
SNOWFLAKE_ACCOUNT=your_account_here
SNOWFLAKE_USER=your_user_here
SNOWFLAKE_DATABASE=your_database_here
SNOWFLAKE_SCHEMA=RAW
SNOWFLAKE_WAREHOUSE=your_warehouse_here

# Pick ONE auth path. Key-pair is recommended, and is REQUIRED if the
# account has MFA enforced -- the connector cannot answer an interactive
# MFA prompt, so password auth simply fails there.
#   SETUP.md section 4 -> "Authentication: key-pair (recommended) vs password"
#
# Path A, key-pair. Point this at your private key file.
SNOWFLAKE_PRIVATE_KEY_PATH=/path/to/your/.snowflake_rsa_key.p8
# Only for an encrypted key.
# SNOWFLAKE_PRIVATE_KEY_PASSPHRASE=only_set_for_encrypted_keys
#
# Path B, password. Leave SNOWFLAKE_PRIVATE_KEY_PATH unset or empty and
# the connector falls back to this.
# SNOWFLAKE_PASSWORD=your_password_here

# The output scripts read from the schema dbt builds into, which defaults
# to ANALYTICS. Set this only if your dbt target schema has another name.
#   SETUP.md section 6 -> "Configure `.env`"
# SNOWFLAKE_ANALYTICS_SCHEMA=ANALYTICS

# --------------------------------------------------------------------
# Google Sheets -- entirely optional
# --------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Leave all of this blank and every output script runs preview-only,
# writing files instead of touching a Sheet. Nothing in QUICKSTART.md
# needs it.

# Path to the OAuth client JSON from your own GCP project.
#   SETUP.md section 10 -> "Create a GCP project + OAuth client"
GOOGLE_OAUTH_CLIENT_PATH=

# MLB-209. A SECOND OAuth client, for the path that creates its own
# workbook instead of opening one you already made. It requests only
# drive.file (non-sensitive: the app sees the files it creates and
# nothing else) and caches its grant separately, so the two never mix.
# Needed only by `generate_almanac_sheet.py --new-public-workbook`;
# leave blank and every existing path is unaffected.
GOOGLE_PUBLIC_OAUTH_CLIENT_PATH=

# Both are Sheet IDs, the /d/<THIS_PART>/edit chunk of a share URL. The
# scripts write to DEV by default and to PROD only when run with --prod,
# so keep a throwaway sheet in DEV and your real league sheet in PROD.
#   SETUP.md section 10 -> "Create the target Sheet", then "Add the env vars"
SHEETS_DEV_ID=
SHEETS_PROD_ID=

# Legacy. Still honored as a fallback for --prod when SHEETS_PROD_ID is
# unset, so older .env files keep working. Prefer SHEETS_PROD_ID for a
# new setup, and note that a value here is a PRODUCTION target rather
# than a spare slot -- whatever sheet it names is what --prod writes to.
# SHEETS_OUTPUT_ID=

# --------------------------------------------------------------------
# Everything below is off the quickstart path
# --------------------------------------------------------------------

# A second, CBS-hosted league. Not part of QUICKSTART.md: the CBS login
# is reCAPTCHA-walled, so there is no scripted credential flow and every
# value here is browser-extracted by hand. The capture scripts treat
# these leagues as read-only.
#   SETUP.md section 6 -> "Configure `.env`", the CBS league block
# CBS_LEAGUE=
# CBS_TOKEN=
# CBS_WEB_COOKIES=
# CBS_SHEETS_DEV_ID=
# CBS_SHEETS_OUTPUT_ID=

# DuckDB target knobs. The transform layer builds on DuckDB as well as
# Snowflake, but nothing lands RAW there, so these are for working
# against a warehouse you already built rather than a way to skip
# Snowflake. Both have working defaults; set them only to override.
#   ALMANAC_DB_BACKEND=duckdb makes the output scripts read DuckDB
#   without passing --duckdb on every call.
#   DBT_DUCKDB_PATH points at the database file.
# ALMANAC_DB_BACKEND=
# DBT_DUCKDB_PATH=
